Q: What should be in PHP&#39;s SessionIdInterface::create_sid() method? A: <p>The manual actually states clearly:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and the return value should be a string that is a valid session ID <strong>for your handler</strong>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>To be more specific, the rules for session id are determined by the party creating the session handler. It can be a random string, a primary field in the database, or even a duplicated session id, as long as you can handle it.</p>
<p>If no special requirements, you can let your handler class inherit the <code>SessionHandler</code> class, which contains a default <code>create_sid</code> method. The difference between it and your implementation is that <code>session_create_id</code> can specify a prefix.</p>
<p>Additionally, if the sid is randomly generated and you need to ensure uniqueness, you can follow the practice of the file session handler, try several times in the <code>create_sid</code> method:</p>
<pre><code>public function create_sid(){
    $retry = 3;
    do {
        $sid = $this-&gt;generateRandomSid();
        if($this-&gt;validateId($sid))
            return $sid;
    } while(--$retry &gt;= 0);
    return null;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Since you are creating a custom DB session handler, you can actually insert a session record into the DB first, and then return the last inserted ID.</p>
<pre><code>public function create_sid(){
    $this-&gt;db-&gt;exec(...);
    return $this-&gt;db-&gt;lastInsertId();
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to include and use OpenCv3.1.0 library to CUDA file(.cu)? A: <p>Despite the other good answer that suggests separation between C++ and CUDA, there is an alternative way to include OpenCV containers in <code>.cu</code> files:</p>

<p>CMakeLists.txt</p>

<pre><code>cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.8)
project(test LANGUAGES CXX CUDA)
find_package(OpenCV 3.0 REQUIRED)

# compile the target
add_executable(test_app main.cpp medianFilter.cu)
target_link_libraries(test_app PRIVATE cudart ${OpenCV_LIBS})
</code></pre>

<p>main.cpp</p>

<pre><code>#include "opencv2/opencv.hpp"
#include "medianFilter.h"

int main()
{
    // input data
    cv::Mat inputMat(cv::Size(128, 128), CV_8UC3, cv::Scalar(100));
    cv::Mat kernelMat(cv::Size(16, 16), CV_8UC1, cv::Scalar(1));

    // call CUDA
    cudaMedianCaller(inputMat, kernelMat);
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

<p>medianFilter.cu</p>

<pre><code>#include "medianFilter.h"
__global__ void kernelMedianFilter(uchar3* d_inputMat, uchar* d_kernelMat)
{
    return;
}
void cudaMedianCaller(const cv::Mat&amp; inputMat, cv::Mat&amp; kernelMat)
{
    // allocate device pointers
    uchar3 *d_inputMat;
    uchar  *d_kernelMat;
    cudaMalloc(&amp;d_inputMat,  inputMat.total() * sizeof(uchar3));
    cudaMalloc(&amp;d_kernelMat, kernelMat.total() * sizeof(uchar));

    // copy from host to device
    cudaMemcpy(d_inputMat, inputMat.ptr&lt;uchar3&gt;(0), inputMat.total() * sizeof(uchar3), cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);
    cudaMemcpy(d_kernelMat, kernelMat.ptr&lt;uchar&gt;(0), kernelMat.total() * sizeof(uchar), cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);

    // call CUDA kernel
    kernelMedianFilter &lt;&lt;&lt;1, 1&gt;&gt;&gt; (d_inputMat, d_kernelMat);

    // free
    cudaFree(d_inputMat);
    cudaFree(d_kernelMat);
}
</code></pre>

<p>medianFilter.h</p>

<pre><code>#include "opencv2/opencv.hpp"
void cudaMedianCaller (const cv::Mat&amp; inputMat, cv::Mat&amp; kernelMat);
</code></pre>

<p>For the binary application to run, you may need to copy some of the required <code>.dll</code> to the binary folder. For me, I copied <code>opencv_core343.dll</code> from <code>C:\Program Files\OpenCV\x64\vc15\bin</code> to the folder where <code>test_app.exe</code> exists.</p>
